---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { weaponTiers, PATCH } from '../../data/game.js';
---
<ContentLayout
  title="Weapon Tier List"
  description="ARC Raiders weapon tier list: every gun ranked S through C with crafting costs, blueprint requirements, and the balance changes that moved them."
  eyebrow="Weapons"
  heading="Weapon Tier List"
  lede="Roughly two dozen weapons across six ammo types. Ranked here on three things at once — PvP performance, effectiveness against ARC armor, and what it costs you to put one in your hands."
>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Patch baseline: {PATCH.label}.</strong> Weapon balance moves often.
      Rankings that ignore the Kettle nerfs or the 1.17.0 rebalance are stale —
      check the date on any tier list before trusting it, including this one.
    </p>
  </div>

  {weaponTiers.map(tier => (
    <section class="tier-block">
      <div class="tier-head">
        <div class="tier-badge" style={`color:${tier.color}; border-color:${tier.color}`}>{tier.rank}</div>
        <p class="tier-summary">{tier.summary}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Weapon</th><th>Type</th><th>Why it lands here</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {tier.weapons.map(w => (
              <tr><td><strong>{w.name}</strong></td><td>{w.type}</td><td>{w.note}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>How to actually get these</h2>
  <p>
    Weapons split into two groups. A handful — the Ferro and Stitcher among them —
    need no blueprint and can be crafted from the moment you start, which is why
    the Ferro dominates value rankings. The majority are blueprint-dependent:
    you have to find the blueprint before crafting unlocks at all. Blueprints
    drop in raids, and some weapons like the Anvil can be bought outright from a
    dealer if the blueprint refuses to appear.
  </p>
  <p>
    Raise your Gunsmith level to access higher weapon ranks. Rank IV variants are
    meaningfully different weapons, not marginal upgrades — a Tempest at Rank IV
    with a Tier 3 medium magazine is a different gun from the one you first found.
  </p>

  <h2>Do not judge by rarity</h2>
  <p>
    The clearest example is the Ferro: a cheap, common, blueprint-free battle
    rifle that outperforms several Rare and Epic weapons. Meanwhile the Hullcracker
    deals no damage at all to other players despite being an excellent tool, and
    the Equalizer's visible beam gives your position away to everything nearby.
    Rarity describes acquisition cost, not raid performance.
  </p>

  <h2>Recommended starting pairs</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Primary</th><th>Secondary</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>First raids</strong></td><td>Ferro</td><td>Stitcher</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Once heavy ammo is sustainable</strong></td><td>Ferro or Renegade</td><td>Anvil</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>ARC-heavy runs</strong></td><td>Hullcracker</td><td>Anvil</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>PvP-focused</strong></td><td>Tempest or Bobcat</td><td>Anvil</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .tier-block { margin-bottom: 44px; }
  .tier-head { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 20px; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .tier-badge {
    width: 58px; height: 58px; flex-shrink: 0;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700;
    border: 2px solid;
  }
  .tier-summary { margin: 0; font-size: 0.98rem; }
</style>
